   "script": "Today I’d like to use technological progress and risk to illustrate a broader point about evidence. A common starting assumption is that technological progress can create large social benefits while also generating new risks faster than institutions can adapt. Now, notice that this statement contains two parts: a claim about an outcome, and an explanation for why that outcome occurs. The explanation is important. New technologies change costs and capabilities. These changes redistribute power, create new markets and sometimes make old rules ineffective. Researchers then ask whether the predicted pattern appears in data. Communication technologies lower the cost of sharing information, but the same systems can amplify fraud, surveillance and misinformation. But this is where careful interpretation becomes necessary. Regulation introduced too early may freeze an immature technology, while regulation introduced too late may allow harmful practices to become entrenched. So, if you were designing a policy or making a personal decision, the safest conclusion would not be an absolute rule. Instead, Adaptive governance uses transparent standards, monitoring and revision rather than assuming that one rule will remain optimal. For your notes, remember three signposts from this talk: first, identify the mechanism; second, separate association from causation; and third, look for the boundary conditions. Those three questions are useful not only in research, but also when you evaluate claims in everyday life.",
