   "script": "Today I’d like to use scientific change and evidence to illustrate a broader point about evidence. A common starting assumption is that scientific progress is rarely a simple accumulation of facts; new measurements matter, but new concepts can also change what researchers notice and how evidence is interpreted. Now, notice that this statement contains two parts: a claim about an outcome, and an explanation for why that outcome occurs. The explanation is important. Theories guide questions, measurements and expectations. When anomalies accumulate, researchers may revise assumptions, develop better instruments or adopt a different explanatory framework. Researchers then ask whether the predicted pattern appears in data. Many scientific shifts involved both improved data and a new way of organising previously puzzling observations. But this is where careful interpretation becomes necessary. A new theory is not automatically better because it is novel. It must explain existing evidence, make useful predictions and survive independent testing. So, if you were designing a policy or making a personal decision, the safest conclusion would not be an absolute rule. Instead, Science education should therefore teach uncertainty, revision and evidence evaluation, not only final textbook conclusions. For your notes, remember three signposts from this talk: first, identify the mechanism; second, separate association from causation; and third, look for the boundary conditions. Those three questions are useful not only in research, but also when you evaluate claims in everyday life.",
