   "script": "Today I’d like to use art, culture and the brain to illustrate a broader point about evidence. A common starting assumption is that neuroscience can explain some mechanisms of perception and reward during aesthetic experience, but it cannot fully replace cultural and personal interpretations of art. Now, notice that this statement contains two parts: a claim about an outcome, and an explanation for why that outcome occurs. The explanation is important. Visual and auditory patterns engage sensory, memory, emotion and reward systems. Expectations shaped by culture and expertise influence what people notice and value. Researchers then ask whether the predicted pattern appears in data. The same artwork can produce different responses depending on context, familiarity and prior knowledge, showing that experience is not determined by stimulus features alone. But this is where careful interpretation becomes necessary. Brain measures can identify correlates of preference without explaining why a work matters historically or morally. So, if you were designing a policy or making a personal decision, the safest conclusion would not be an absolute rule. Instead, Scientific and humanistic approaches answer different but complementary questions about art. For your notes, remember three signposts from this talk: first, identify the mechanism; second, separate association from causation; and third, look for the boundary conditions. Those three questions are useful not only in research, but also when you evaluate claims in everyday life.",
